# Database imports restricted to services

- Only `packages/services` and `packages/db` may import `@dg/db` or `@dg/db/testing`.
- `apps/*` (including tests and `jest.config.ts`) and other packages must NOT import `@dg/db` or `@dg/db/testing`.
- Instead, expose database operations through `packages/services` functions and mock those service functions in `apps/*` tests.

## Why

- Keeps database logic centralized and testable in `packages/services`.
- Prevents apps from coupling directly to the ORM.
- Ensures `@dg/web#test` runs purely in memory without waking the `DATABASE_URL_TEST` Neon compute endpoint or colliding with `@dg/services#test` in CI.

## Example

```ts
// ❌ Bad - app importing db directly
// apps/web/src/services/strava.ts
import { db } from '@dg/db';
const token = await db.Token.findOne({ where: { name: 'strava' } });

// ✅ Good - app imports from services
// apps/web/src/services/strava.ts
import { getStravaOauthStatus } from '@dg/services/strava/getStravaOauthStatus';
const status = await getStravaOauthStatus();
```

## Testing

- Only `packages/services` tests may import `@dg/db` or `@dg/db/testing` (for example `setupTestDatabase` from `@dg/db/testing/databaseSetup` and `dbConfig` from `@dg/db/testing/jest.config.base`).
- `apps/*` tests and `apps/*/jest.config.ts` must NEVER import `@dg/db` or `@dg/db/testing`; use `@dg/testing/jest.config.base` and mock `@dg/services/*` boundaries instead.

```ts
// ❌ Bad - app runtime or app test importing db or db/testing
import { db } from '@dg/db';
import { setupTestDatabase } from '@dg/db/testing/databaseSetup';

// ✅ Good - packages/services tests using db testing helpers
// packages/services/spotify/__tests__/syncSpotifyHistory.test.ts
import { setupTestDatabase } from '@dg/db/testing/databaseSetup';
```
